BUG: `secrets copy` hangs on pip (X11) — reproduced 2026-08-16.

SYMPTOM: `secrets copy /alerting/slack-webhook-url` never returns. No output,
no error. From a non-interactive caller it blocks indefinitely (>120s observed,
had to kill it). The secret IS fetched and IS placed on the clipboard — it is
purely the return that never happens.

CAUSE (super/bin/secrets, copy_to_clipboard(), lines ~53-55):

    printf '%s' "$value" | xclip -selection clipboard 2>/dev/null
    printf '%s' "$value" | xclip -selection primary 2>/dev/null || true

xclip forks a daemon to hold the X selection (X11 requires the owning client to
stay alive to serve paste requests — unlike wl-copy, which detaches cleanly).
That daemon INHERITS the caller's stdout/stderr, so the pipe never sees EOF and
the caller blocks until some other client takes the selection. Interactively you
may get your prompt back and not notice; any non-interactive caller hangs.

pip is X11 (XDG_SESSION_TYPE=x11, WAYLAND_DISPLAY unset) and has ONLY xclip
installed (no wl-copy, no xsel) — so pip takes the xclip branch every time. The
Wayland branch above it is clean, which is likely why this went unnoticed.

FIX: detach xclip and close its inherited streams, e.g.

    printf '%s' "$value" | xclip -selection clipboard >/dev/null 2>&1 &
    disown

(or add -quiet and background it). Note 2>/dev/null alone is NOT enough — stdout
is inherited too, and that is what holds the pipeline open. The xsel branch has
the same hazard in principle; xsel needs --nodetach handling or the same
treatment.

WORKAROUND until fixed: `secrets get <path>` works fine, but prints the
plaintext to screen/transcript, which is exactly what `copy` exists to avoid.
So on pip there is currently NO safe way to get a secret to the clipboard.

WIDER POINT worth a look while in there: this is a house tool whose failure mode
is a HANG rather than an error, so it fails silently in scripts and in AI
sessions. Worth checking the rest of super/bin for other 'fork a daemon that
inherits stdout' patterns (anything piping into xclip/xsel/at/setsid).

CROSS-REF: this is the SAME bug independently reported from the ansible strand
2026-08-16 as `20260816T120844Z-UjmySL` (hit while getting an SMB password onto
an Android phone for VLC). That report has the impact framing — `secrets hints`
opens by recommending `secrets copy` precisely so plaintext never lands in a
transcript the OpenSearch archive ingests, so the broken tool is the one that
exists to prevent printing secrets, and the natural workaround is the behaviour
it guards against. It guessed the cause (X11 selection owner / clipboard
manager) but did not confirm it. THIS report has the confirmed cause and the
fix. Triage them together — one fix closes both.
